  "limitations": [
    "Study 1 archival data was limited to 15th-19th centuries to avoid confounding effects of later artistic movements and cross-cultural influences.",
    "Study 2 participants were university students, not formally trained artists or photographers, which might limit generalizability of artistic production styles.",
    "Study 3 found some statistical interactions involving gender or ethnicity of models with culture, but authors stated these did not qualify the qualitative pattern of means.",
    "The study's focus on East Asians (China, Korea, Japan) and Westerners (Americans) limits generalizability to other cultures.",
    "The study acknowledges that artistic conventions do not necessarily correspond to preferences but may simply reflect habits or associations, which the study aimed to address by directly testing preferences."
  ],
  "minimum_safe_summary": "This paper investigates cultural differences in aesthetic preferences and artistic styles between East Asians and Americans, linking them to established cognitive differences in attention (holistic vs. analytic). Across three studies—an archival analysis of traditional art, an experiment on contemporary drawing and photography, and a preference task for portrait photographs—the authors consistently found that East Asians favor context-inclusive visual representations with smaller focal objects and higher horizons, while Americans prefer object-focused representations. These findings suggest that culturally shaped attentional patterns are internalized and manifest in both artistic production and aesthetic judgment. Limitations include the specific historical period for archival data and the student population for experimental studies.",
